# ---- the MEM cell of the running jobs of the caller -------------------------
# Form b, the default: used/allocated, both in the unit of the allocation,
# binary: M below 1000 MiB, T from 1 TiB, else G.  A whole allocation prints
# whole and its use as ever: from 10 up whole, below that with one decimal and
# no ".0".  An allocation that is not whole prints with one decimal, cut, never
# rounded up (15600M is 15.2G), and its use with one decimal too.  Within that
# decimal form a job under its allocation never reads as much (one step, 0.1,
# below it), nor one at or over it less.  The used part is padded to the
# allocated figure + 2 (+ 1 with a decimal: either way a use up to ten times the
# allocation), so a cell is as wide as its allocation makes it, whatever the
# reading, and nothing moves under watch.  WHOLE is the narrower fallback for a
# screen: an allocation from 10 up that is not whole is cut to a whole number
# and its use cut the same way, with no guard (15/15G at 99 %: the colour says
# the rest).  MARK ("?" no reading, "!" a failed one) stands in for the use.
